status: CLOSED (user, 2026-10-18) -- not planned. The derive would only
generate the 4-line manual `impl Debug` shown below, and still needs the
trait (and usually an explicit `impl MyDebugTrait for A`) written by hand,
so total code grows where it is used. `{::TraitName}` (the custom printer)
already covers custom formatting at the call site. The one effect it has
that `{::Trait}` does not: it redirects *all* Debug formatting for the type
(panic messages, std containers), silently. The syntax also forces a
same-unit lookup of the trait just to learn the delegate method's name.
Revisit only in the inverted form -- `#derive(Hex)` deriving a user printer
trait with the standard field dump, so `{::Hex}` works on every opted-in
type without hand-written formatters. Today
`#derive(Debug = MyDebugTrait)` panics "derive expr Debug = MyDebugTrait".
See handoff.md "Known open holes".

#derive(Debug = MyDebugTrait)
struct A{}

trait MyDebugTrait{
  func debug2(self, f: Fmt*);
}

//manualy
impl Debug for A{
  func debug(self, f: Fmt*){ 
    print("delegate\n");
    MyDebugTrait::debug2(self, f);//derived
  }
}
